earcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-1891ccee42c94070a67c404fadf56600", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-1891ccee42c94070a67c404fadf56600", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Identifying Rare Patterns in a Tabular Dataset Using Docta\n\n## 1. Research question and operational definition of \"rare pattern\"\n\n**Question (restated).** How can Docta be used to identify rare patterns in a tabular dataset \u2014 i.e., which Docta-backed procedure (column-tail profiling, anomaly/outlier scoring, or co-occurrence/association framing) most reliably surfaces genuinely rare value-patterns, and with what thresholding and validation?\n\n**Operational definition of \"rare pattern\" (accepted from plan, A1).** A pattern is a value-tuple over a chosen column subset S (1 \u2264 |S| \u2264 W) observed in a set of rows R. It is a *candidate rare pattern* if it satisfies:\n\n- **Support condition:** relative frequency `s = |R|/N \u2264 \u03c4` AND absolute count `|R| \u2265 k` (\u03c4 relative threshold, k minimum absolute count); and/or\n- **Deviation condition:** observed-vs-expected deviation from marginal independence exceeds a residual threshold \u03b2, using the standardized residual `z = (obs \u2212 exp)/sqrt(exp)` (or a Binomial/Poisson tail p-value), where `exp` is the product of marginal frequencies over the columns in S.\n\n\u03c4, k, and \u03b2 are **tunable parameters** with explicit sweep ranges (see Controls). A pattern is *genuinely rare and meaningful* only if it passes the support threshold AND a deviation test calibrated against an independence/permutation null (Section 4), with a family-wise error correction across all tested column subsets.\n\n## 2. Design and rationale\n\n### 2a. Dataset: seeded synthetic tabular data with ground truth\n\nNo dataset exists in the task environment (`datasets=[]`), so the experiment uses a **parameterized synthetic generator** producing a mixed-type table: numeric, categorical, datetime, boolean, and missing-value columns; scale ~10\u2074\u201310\u2076 rows \u00d7 5\u201350 columns. The generator is fully seeded (fixed RNG) and writes, alongside the table, a **latent ground-truth annotation**: pattern id, columns involved, value tuple, number of injected rows, and a per-row boolean mask. This makes detection quality exactly measurable.\n\n**Planted pattern types (enumerated) and how each is generated:**\n- **P1 \u2014 Extreme numeric tails.** Base columns drawn i.i.d. (e.g., Normal, shifted-Exponential, heavy tail). Injection: overwrite `n_i` rows with values beyond the 99.5th percentile of the base distribution (or a fixed out-of-range constant). Tests strategy S1 numeric tails.\n- **P2 \u2014 Rare categorical values.** Inject a value with target frequency 0.1\u20135% (swept) into a high-cardinality and separately a low-cardinality column. Tests S1 freq-table rare categories.\n- **P3 \u2014 Rare multi-column combinations (individually common, jointly rare).** Pick values `a` in column A and `b` in column B that each occur individually with non-trivial marginal frequency, but inject the joint pair at very low rate, violating independence (lift >> 1 at low support). Tests S3 co-occurrence framing.\n- **P4 \u2014 Near-duplicate / duplicate blocks.** Replicate an existing row pattern `n_i` times (exact and near-identical copies with small noise). Tests near-duplicate detection in any strategy.\n- **P5 \u2014 Rare segment / drift.** A time-ordered column with a contiguous injected block where one column's distribution shifts (or a rare joint combination appears). Only visible via row subsetting. Tests the \"pattern over subsets\" interpretation.\n- **P6 \u2014 Co-missing pattern.** Rows with coincident missingness across a correlated set of columns, where each column's overall missing rate is low (\u22642%). Rare \"co-missingness\" pattern. Tests S1 missingness profiling.\n- **P7 \u2014 \"Needle\" pattern.** An extremely rare single value (1\u20135 rows) in an otherwise clean column \u2014 the hard case probing the \u03c4/k floor.\n\n**Rationale.** Ground-truth-seeded synthetic data is the only way to measure precision/recall of \"rareness\" detection without labeled real data, and it lets rarity levels, cardinalities, and column counts be swept to characterize the boundary of detectability. Mixed types exercise Docta's dtype handling.\n\n### 2b. Detection strategies mapped onto Docta, with per-feature availability matrix\n\n**API placeholder policy:** because no Docta package/docs exist here, all references to Docta calls are written as clearly named placeholders using `DOCTA_<CAPABILITY>(...)` with `<...>` for unpinnable arguments. **These are placeholders, not confirmed function names/APIs.** Any metric computed from Docta profile output or from raw data by our own code is labeled **derived**; nothing is ever attributed to Docta as a native capability unless the availability matrix marks it \"native (assumed).\"\n\n**Strategy S1 \u2014 Column profiling + distribution tails.** Relies on a Docta per-column profiling output: distinct/cardinality, frequency table, percentiles/quantiles, min/max, skew, missing rate.\n- Procedure: (a) rare categorical values: from the frequency table, flag values with count in [k, \u03c4\u00b7N]; (b) numeric tails: flag rows outside Tukey fences (Q1\u22121.5\u00b7IQR, Q3+1.5\u00b7IQR) and outside percentile band; (c) missingness: flag columns/rows with unexpected coincident missingness from the missing-rate profile.\n\n**Strategy S2 \u2014 Anomaly/outlier scoring.** Relies on any per-value/per-row anomaly or deviation score Docta may expose, or schema/domain validation that flags never-seen or out-of-domain values as novel.\n- Procedure: obtain per-row/value scores; rank by score; flag top-K / score above percentile threshold; report whether scores originate from a native Docta anomaly API (native) or from residuals computed from profile statistics (derived).\n\n**Strategy S3 \u2014 Co-occurrence / association framing.** Relies on profile marginal frequencies to compute expected joint frequencies; optionally on any native association/co-occurrence output Docta exposes.\n- Procedure: enumerate candidate column subsets (width 1, 2, then 3; width limited for feasibility); compute observed joint frequency from the table; compute expected joint frequency as product of marginal frequencies (from profile freq tables, or derived directly from data); compute lift = obs/exp, standardized residual z, and one-tailed probability under Binomial/Poisson; flag combinations with lift \u2265 \u03c1, support in [k, \u03c4\u00b7N], and significant tail p after correction.\n\n**Docta-availability matrix (per feature: available \u2192 procedure; unavailable \u2192 fallback):**\n\n| Required capability | If available in Docta (native, assumed) | If unavailable \u2192 fallback |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) Per-column profile incl. frequency table | S1 categorical rare values, S3 expected marginals via `DOCTA_PROFILE(...)` | **Derived:** compute value counts/percentiles directly from the raw table with pandas; relabel every metric \"derived from table via profile-shaped output\" |\n| (b) Percentile/quantile/IQR stats | S1 numeric tails via `DOCTA_PROFILE(...)` | **Derived:** compute Q1/Q3/IQR/percentiles ourselves from the raw column |\n| (c) Per-value / per-row anomaly score | S2 uses native `DOCTA_ANOMALY(...)` (placeholder) score directly | **Derived:** standardized residual z = (x\u2212mean)/sd (or robust MAD-median) computed from profile/table; marked derived |\n| (d) Schema/enum/domain validation (novel/never-seen) | S2 flags out-of-declared-domain values as novel via `DOCTA_SCHEMA_VALIDATE(...)` | **Derived:** treat values with zero count in a learned profile enum as novel; mark derived |\n| (e) Native association/rule/pattern mining | S3 uses native co-occurrence output directly | **Derived:** S3 residual/lift computed by our code over column subsets; mark derived |\n| (f) Missingness profile (per-column and coincident) | S1 missing-rate and co-missing detection via `DOCTA_PROFILE(...)` | **Derived:** missing-rate from table; co-missing via our co-occurrence residual over missing-indicator columns |\n| (g) No Docta at all / install fails | \u2014 | **Not possible:** the question is \"using Docta\"; a Docta-less run cannot answer it. Contingency: document the exact place in the pipeline where Docta would plug in, and stop (no detection claim). |\n\n### 2c. Pipeline\n\nSetup (install/pin `DOCTA_VERSION`, pandas, numpy, scipy) \u2192 generate seeded base table + null table \u2192 inject patterns per seed \u2192 run S1/S2/S3 pipelines over all columns/subset combinations with raw threshold set \u2192 compute ranked candidate-pattern lists \u2192 evaluate against ground truth \u2192 sweep \u03c4/k/\u03b2 \u2192 report metric surfaces and decision.\n\n## 3. Controls\n\n- **Null dataset:** the identical generator with zero planted patterns and matched marginals. Every strategy is run on it to measure the **false-positive baseline** (FPR, number of spurious candidates) and to build the **empirical null distribution** of residual/lift scores under the independence model.\n- **Threshold sweeps:** \u03c4 \u2208 {0.05%, 0.1%, 0.5%, 1%, 2%}; k \u2208 {1, 2, 5, 10}; residual \u03b2 \u2208 {2, 3, 5} or p \u2208 {0.01, 0.001, 0.0001}; lift \u03c1 \u2208 {1.5, 3, 5, 10}. Reported as metric surfaces over (\u03c4,k) per strategy.\n- **Multiple-comparison correction:** Benjamini\u2013Hochberg FDR across all tested column-subset combinations before any candidate is called significant.\n- **Independence / permutation baseline:** for each combination, expected joint frequency under the product-of-marginals null; a two-sided permutation test (shuffle column values to break joint structure) provides a nonparametric null; Binomial/Poisson tail p-values provide the parametric check. A candidate is \"genuinely rare\" only if its support is \u2264 \u03c4N, \u2265 k, and its deviation p-value survives correction against this null.\n- **Reproducibility:** fixed RNG seeds, repeated injection sampling across \u2265 5 seeds, bootstrap CIs on metrics; a stability split (train on one seed's thresholds, evaluate on held-out seeds) to show thresholds are not overfit to a single dataset.\n\n## 4. Analysis plan\n\n**Exact metrics:**\n- **Precision / Recall / F1** per strategy per threshold (TP = candidate matching a planted pattern-id; FP = candidate not in ground truth; FN = planted pattern not surfaced).\n- **Recall@K:** fraction of planted ground-truth patterns contained in the top-K ranked candidate list (ranking by p-value / residual / anomaly score).\n- **False-positive rate (FPR)** at each threshold on the null dataset; **lift and O-vs-E significance** (z, binomial/p-value) per candidate as the rareness evidence itself.\n- **Ranking quality:** AUPRC (precision-recall curve over the candidate ranking) and/or NDCG over the ground-truth order; mean reciprocal rank of each planted pattern.\n- **Runtime/feasibility:** rows/sec per strategy, scaling with N and with subset width W (S3 enumeration cost for 1-, 2-, 3-wide subsets is the dominant term; capped and documented).\n\n**Decision rule for which strategy best surfaces rare patterns:** compare strategies on the primary metric **Recall@K and AUPRC at a controlled FPR \u2264 5%** on the null dataset across the \u03c4/k sweep. A strategy (or hybrid S1+S3 ensemble flagged as derived) is judged best if it achieves significantly higher AUPRC and stable recall@K across seeds (paired bootstrap confidence intervals), and if its surfaced candidates on the null set remain at/below the FPR budget at the chosen thresholds. The final answer reports, per strategy: the thresholds chosen, which planted pattern types (P1\u2013P7) it recovers vs. misses (e.g., S1 excels at P1/P2/P6, S3 at P3/P7, S2 at novel-value P2), and which step relied on native Docta vs. derived output.\n\n**Runtime/feasibility assumptions:** pandas-based implementation; subset enumeration capped (e.g., all 1- and 2-way, sample of 3-way); the environment nominally exposes 224 CPUs if a run were ever permitted, but no run is executed here.\n\n## 5. Feasibility statement (plain)\n\n**This is design-only. No experiment was run, and none is possible in this environment**: there is no Docta package, no Docta documentation or source, no network access, and even the supporting Python data stack is absent (verified: `import docta` \u2192 ModuleNotFoundError; `pip list` shows no pandas/numpy/sklearn; Python 3.14.4 system-only). Therefore **no detection-performance claims are made and none should be inferred**; all Docta API references are placeholders. No dataset was provided, so the synthetic generator above is part of the design, not a performed data step.\n\n**Minimal environment needed to actually run this:** Linux/macOS with Python \u2265 3.9; `pandas`, `numpy`, `scipy`; Docta pinned at a specific version (`DOCTA_VERSION` \u2014 to be locked from its release metadata at run time, since it is not available here); CPU-only compute is sufficient; network only needed for the initial `pip install` of Docta. All code must be runnable by another party by resolving the named placeholders (`DOCTA_PROFILE`, `DOCTA_ANOMALY`, `DOCTA_SCHEMA_VALIDATE`, and any association-mining capability) against the pinned Docta's actual API, with the availability matrix deciding native-vs-derived labeling.\n\n## Conclusion preview (do not overstate)\nSubject to the availability matrix, the design anticipates that the strongest, self-contained answer will be: **use Docta's per-column profile (frequency tables, percentiles, missingness) as the basis, then compute derived rare-category, extreme-tail, and co-occurrence residual/lift scores on top of it, validated by an independence/permutation null with FDR correction** \u2014 with native Docta anomaly/association capabilities used wherever available and labeled accordingly. This framing stays honest to the environment: Docta is treated as the profiling substrate, and every pattern-surfacing statistic beyond that substrate is explicitly labeled derived.", "evidence": "Inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (363 B; question, context, documents.track.md note 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'); track.md not physically present. `find / -iname '*docta*'` \u2192 no results; `python3 -c 'import docta'` \u2192 ModuleNotFoundError; `pip list` (system, Python 3.14.4) contains no pandas/numpy/sklearn and no Docta; env has no network/proxy config and the environment spec declares network=disabled, datasets=[], gpu=false. All Docta capability claims are therefore capability-agnostic placeholders (DOCTA_<CAPABILITY>), not confirmed APIs. Researcher approved plan (approach_approved=true), confirming A1 (rare-pattern operationalization with \u03c4/k/\u03b2 tuning), A3 (native vs derived labeling), and the per-feature fallback approach.", "assumptions": ["A1 (approved): 'Rare pattern' = value/value-combination over column subset S with relative frequency s <= \u03c4 (\u03c4 in {0.05%,0.1%,0.5%,1%,2%}) and absolute count >= k (k in {1,2,5,10}), and/or observed-vs-expected deviation from marginal independence above residual threshold \u03b2 (derived metrics labeled as such).", "A2: no dataset is provided in the environment; a fully seeded synthetic mixed-type tabular generator (mixed dtypes, ~1e4-1e6 rows x 5-50 cols) with planted ground-truth patterns (P1-P7) is assumed and is part of the design.", "A3 (approved): Docta provides a programmatic per-column profile whose output (freq table, percentiles, cardinality, missing rate) is the substrate for all strategies; every downstream metric not natively provided by Docta is explicitly labeled 'derived'.", "A4: patterns are detected at the value/value-combination and tail level without labels or target colu